                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{p:\mathbb{Q}} \prod_{q:\mathbb{Q}} (p \lt q) \to \sum_{b:\mathrm{bool}} ((b = 0) \to (p \lt r)) \times ((b = 1) \to (r \lt q))
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A locator is equivalent to having the structure of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
                with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulus+of+convergence"} modulus of convergence
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulated+Cauchy+real+number"} modulated Cauchy real number
            %li
              %p
                That every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+number"} Dedekind real number
                has a
                = succeed "-indexed" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℚ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+principle+of+omniscience"} limited principle of omniscience
          %h2#principle_of_locators Principle of locators
          %p Note: The “principle of locators” or “axiom of locators” are placeholder names for a principle or axiom which may or may not have an already existing name in the mathematics literature.
          %p
            The
            %strong principle of locators
            state that every real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:\mathbb{R}
            merely has a locator (i.e. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support"} support
            of the locator has an element), and implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+numbers"} Cauchy real numbers
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+field"} Archimedean ordered field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            which also uses
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
          %p
            However, in general constructive mathematics, while theorem 6.10.3 of
            %a{:href => "#Booij20"} Booij 2020
            states that a real number is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+number"} Cauchy real number
            if and only if it merely has a locator, not every real number is necessarily a Cauchy real number, so it is not necessarily true that every real number has a locator. In that case, this principle becomes the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong axiom of locators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:\mathbb{R}
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+field"} Archimedean ordered field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            merely has a locator, making it coincide with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+numbers"} Cauchy real numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            may not exist.
